#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c9946da-c8fb-5598-9310-0635636e2435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550291} {:answer/id #uuid "9346b182-534f-5768-a92d-14cb708091d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550297} {:answer/id #uuid "c43cb67e-0e07-53ac-bd2f-e4b46856febf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{6} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550305} {:answer/id #uuid "f41b3a53-1513-5d90-a29e-c0d94361791b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{8} + \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550322} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5eb5b1-cb2e-5bed-a14f-bed3327759bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550326} :answer-field/id #uuid "92f58760-2c63-5481-aed7-155b18d6271e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cece6418-e25b-52c0-84e8-9f3f46d2fb2f" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\qquad v=2 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{7}{2 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=9 & ⟹\\, & u=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{2 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 9,\\,2\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/07/075e4196e1ad4b6dafc70878555598bac64e8880b2c152d61c2a6437acbaaceb.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{12 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{12 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{12 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{4}^{9}\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e2bf6e-19ed-5858-98ed-6d2551df3a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550255} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7f9d50-f67c-5a4a-9290-79eae5d25588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550287} {:answer/id #uuid "92b6032b-6b22-566f-b2c5-42119f8b2493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550295} {:answer/id #uuid "95eca1f7-296e-5899-ba07-f57fd8732853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550298} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a9b54e-4e72-5767-8a68-557209fb6526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550255} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f9d0170-b331-569d-b9e7-07729838c80b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d1dac97a-39b4-5865-ae85-b58196c1a98b" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=3 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x - 4,\\qquad y=2 x + 4,\\qquad y=3 - 3 x,\\qquad y=5 - 3 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=3 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-4 & ⟹\\, & u=-4 \\\\  & y=2 x + 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=5 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/438f7c0ee42f63a596ff65ae9c2b354c93bb2f97ad4d7b5b0a1a161a3c5d173b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{5}=- \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241c0011-aeb6-5715-867f-08dbc7b8365c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550263} {:answer/id #uuid "4d801f08-706a-54dd-8726-8efe2ad05c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550272} {:answer/id #uuid "6d454b96-ad9a-520e-8e70-88ce8979f324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550284} {:answer/id #uuid "8027fd76-5626-52e3-8da9-e75dde48cfb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550288} {:answer/id #uuid "8b509bef-5cb6-5a5d-9aef-179ae2302238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550288} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13d0a1a-602a-5493-aa9b-b98af622ae11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d39c0daa-7f15-5653-beed-dc484aaa4d7c" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=4 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x,\\qquad y=2 x + 3,\\qquad y=1 - 4 x,\\qquad y=5 - 4 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 3 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=5 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d6/d605500af69b1a828496810a93a084b065ec92ebd99b03cbf14a37b03840dd31.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b27fdd-cc9a-5801-b180-01f4a70bc0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{169}+\\frac{16{y}^{2}}{529}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550332}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550332} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e1ae4d-133e-5ac3-84d6-bc430526f02a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1a6b38d4-7f97-5b59-aaba-751584b345ee" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e9a8c7db3d0a811ca1dc95db8060a5488b4b9b2fb47fc08d25f1a959357626e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{13}{3}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{23}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{13}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{23}{4}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{169}{9}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{529}{16}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{169}+\\frac{16{y}^{2}}{529} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca576feb-f650-5bb4-bffb-b567ff06c318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{16{y}^{2}}{441}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550339} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c9691e-11ca-5751-8436-b826f49825f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3bbb5303-3cd6-5c7a-94a7-126cdd38904f" :question/problem "![](images/50/50782efd1f5f15904e974810f5db14eb41ffa9a81f790226cd5048dc33c77a93.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{4}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{21}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{21}{4}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{16}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{441}{16}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{16{y}^{2}}{441} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b083f8-a5ef-562c-9608-019814b62ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16{x}^{2}}{625}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{484}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550338} :answer-field/id #uuid "7913aa25-1f85-5a14-8bd8-8f5d63bb6afc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4d9ef7fc-bb8c-57e4-a59a-c8e3d3a4c0d4" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c61e776c1c576b156b7fae95815459ee91b36cffd3b02ac1610a8293d9d08309.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{25}{4}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{22}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{22}{3}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{625}{16}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{484}{9}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{625}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{484} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f06919c-d2d4-5cd1-ace7-b29ccdfccef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{361}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550329} :answer-field/id #uuid "34754d1c-d6cd-5f2b-ae58-2df8c449139f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77b4bcbd-934f-5219-bfb2-10a129134250" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b629b57b84284cff9d54d9ac47b886be8e77c80cb1cdd3c010bf92dc16dd274.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{10}{3}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{19}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{19}{3}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{100}{9}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{361}{9}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{361} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5abfe5b1-5ae6-559f-9c51-efb17e4c1804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{400}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{529}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550334} :answer-field/id #uuid "d485f67d-1df6-51de-bddc-b8b2e1804ccd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3d99764-38ad-5c62-880d-420b0ba11fa5" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0c9b9b056aa08b09342da5daad12d23c40462d25e1a04088177b51de4f9b6af.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{20}{3}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{23}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{20}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{23}{3}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{400}{9}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{529}{9}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{400}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{529} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac92f55-bdd5-5ebb-8623-7211f79f9620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550395} {:answer/id #uuid "179b5f9d-873a-571a-b6bf-69376a9ef389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550418} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfb5464-e95f-5d2a-b797-775d6cf22682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550431} {:answer/id #uuid "a8026852-ad3d-5420-9b64-1828b556006e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550811} {:answer/id #uuid "d9563607-dd70-5767-87cb-3d112f17eed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550395} :answer-field/id #uuid "be155506-4e79-5a1a-866f-997f1cb30ccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17a8497e-763c-5804-a8ea-e63d106be3fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-125497" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4ee9e02184efd314371a99c6242540e5e2e6effc9e21d41a86648b433b8a6938.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d91a85ccbb948e070db738373ffc5032f4e91a0ba8e04cd14e79276b0e798f8.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e0bffd04fb7b765d3c654f62b1958ab55a42f0d26cbc9f2b2e31f7d8e6f775.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed18d44-37e8-529c-a585-0ceab88f3f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550685} {:answer/id #uuid "8858c48e-583e-5419-89c1-66b239d5970c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550713} {:answer/id #uuid "b2545c5c-af10-54e4-a998-61fc386ed756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550854} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee07b7-d7ad-5975-9eff-46b920985b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550954} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1fa96f-fbba-519c-ac7f-2711d8804739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550685} :answer-field/id #uuid "53503a6a-a2ae-508c-9eef-c15dd0d61993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2310204a-fdf5-57d1-9e96-98189fedc070" :question/math-academy-id "q-125490" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f94742a7f37fd7f6a1c789401a2e323fec80aca584774453cba03cf69f28ade.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4e8c62562ee1657e41bb36a21c901dc95935558641f724f934a4c58706f4a64.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/40/40b100dd4bc71b95f5ad99eb0cec8ed87133211bb371b22deccb851f79c695b3.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0903ffe2-9d1a-51b2-851e-5c852bdde114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551524} {:answer/id #uuid "11dec961-06c9-5500-94ca-ed63d5257fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551525} {:answer/id #uuid "5a64aa87-c9f3-5c7f-9ab4-27eb4b5f084d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551526} {:answer/id #uuid "74259d58-1461-5af2-a1d5-127197a771ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551527} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0a2578-5fab-589e-bc4f-c403a4e7e3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551527} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79e0d9e-181d-57d1-9f9a-a7533fbd082e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a7ad19ee-c937-5a75-b1b6-4803c2c7bf3b" :question/problem "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(- 8 x)\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{C_1}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r+\\int_{C_2}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path $C_1$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$r_1(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j,$$\n\nwith $t$ running from $0$ to $2$. Along this path, $x=t$ and $y=t^{2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$F(r_1(t))=(- 8 t)\\,i+(- 8 t^{2})\\,j.$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$r_1'(t)=(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$F(r_1(t))\\cdot r_1'(t)=(- 8 t)(1)+(- 8 t^{2})(2 t)=- 16 t^{3} - 8 t.$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\\int_{C_1}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{0}^{2}(- 16 t^{3} - 8 t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\left[- 4 t^{4} - 4 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}=-80.$$\n\n$$\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=(-80)+(16)=-64.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b570f2-170f-50b9-b0a5-9563089e0914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-12)\\,\\mathbf i+(-4)\\,\\mathbf j+(-18)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551553} {:answer/id #uuid "85d95e24-11f1-5424-b411-e0cba95ba1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{3}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{3}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551566} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d64b4c-f8eb-5e52-a731-c64e6c9515d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{9}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{3}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{3}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551579} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cf17ec-9cf4-5d4b-ac5b-642ccc70904b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{12}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{4}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{18}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551581} {:answer/id #uuid "eddd4ced-d849-5ca7-ab21-5aa50e4a8fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{30}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{10}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{24}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551581} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14bbab2-5183-5313-8025-08879f6edb60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "01b80278-13c1-5bc7-888c-457bedd82092" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(1,3,1)$ if $g(x,y,z)=- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x + y + z)(- y z)-(- x y z)(3)}{(3 x + y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{y z \\left(y + z\\right)}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x + y + z)(- x z)-(- x y z)(1)}{(3 x + y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{x z \\left(3 x + z\\right)}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x + y + z)(- x y)-(- x y z)(1)}{(3 x + y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{x y \\left(3 x + y\\right)}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(\\frac{3 x y z}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{y z}{3 x + y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(\\frac{x y z}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{x z}{3 x + y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(\\frac{x y z}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{x y}{3 x + y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(1,3,1) & =\\left(\\frac{(7)(-3)-(-3)(3)}{(7)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(7)(-1)-(-3)(1)}{(7)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(7)(-3)-(-3)(1)}{(7)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{4}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{18}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{4}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{18}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f20dac-6288-5051-a0c9-656192ea317e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{54}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{18}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{18}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551535} {:answer/id #uuid "1b82d1e6-c06d-5bc5-a3be-ca74e8368591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{6}{11}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{18}{11}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{6}{11}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551539} {:answer/id #uuid "2beb0246-9a96-5b86-b925-3ee4afed1adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-12)\\,\\mathbf i+(-216)\\,\\mathbf j+(-48)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551541} {:answer/id #uuid "30d13711-2fa2-5805-aef5-71db8eb39744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{12}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{216}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{48}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551545} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e4497f-d6d4-5ce0-a5a1-5acd3a94ffb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{120}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{180}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{84}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551545} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf24e375-8a4c-5062-b9e8-dd5c1bf1791d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d2b5e18-84ca-5425-a8f7-9933f3d81196" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(3,1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z)=- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - y + z)(- 2 y z)-(- 2 x y z)(3)}{(3 x - y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 y z \\left(y - z\\right)}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - y + z)(- 2 x z)-(- 2 x y z)(-1)}{(3 x - y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 x z \\left(3 x + z\\right)}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - y + z)(- 2 x y)-(- 2 x y z)(1)}{(3 x - y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 x y \\left(3 x - y\\right)}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(\\frac{6 x y z}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{2 y z}{3 x - y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(- \\frac{2 x y z}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{2 x z}{3 x - y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(\\frac{2 x y z}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{2 x y}{3 x - y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(3,1,3) & =\\left(\\frac{(11)(-6)-(-18)(3)}{(11)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(11)(-18)-(-18)(-1)}{(11)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(11)(-6)-(-18)(1)}{(11)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{216}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{48}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{216}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{48}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "326a1a4f-342f-55b4-a110-a2709831b255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{11}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551546} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3bfb05-0026-500e-ab07-b20fa3d7a282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{7}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{13}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551555} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3b866f-b033-5d4a-960c-12a16bdfb0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(90)\\,\\mathbf i+(9)\\,\\mathbf j+(-99)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551562} {:answer/id #uuid "890840ec-d66a-5ea2-a525-97e6db70ac5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{3}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551567} {:answer/id #uuid "b54a31e3-2b50-5c38-88f2-32aebf4915c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551546} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5196fb-11c8-5508-80c7-18d7ea226d31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "475d0eee-bd08-5379-9e3a-cbd39068cbc3" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(-1,-3,1)$ if $g(x,y,z)=- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)(- 3 y z)-(- 3 x y z)(-2)}{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 y z \\left(3 y - z\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y - z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)(- 3 x z)-(- 3 x y z)(-3)}{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 x z \\left(2 x - z\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y - z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)(- 3 x y)-(- 3 x y z)(1)}{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 x y \\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y - z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(- \\frac{6 x y z}{\\left(- 2 x - 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{3 y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(- \\frac{9 x y z}{\\left(- 2 x - 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{3 x z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(\\frac{3 x y z}{\\left(- 2 x - 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{3 x y}{- 2 x - 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(-1,-3,1) & =\\left(\\frac{(12)(9)-(-9)(-2)}{(12)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(12)(3)-(-9)(-3)}{(12)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(12)(-9)-(-9)(1)}{(12)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{11}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{11}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2534f636-728f-5cf5-84af-dcdc84233ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(- 6 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(4 z)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551540} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1a9e97-8397-5a96-8f61-336af217a27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(6 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(4 z)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551544} {:answer/id #uuid "62dabc43-5bf0-5679-aa83-c9aa4bc3b568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 6 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(6 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(4 z)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551557} {:answer/id #uuid "9253ad92-4f06-57ac-bdda-8ca58af71ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(2 z)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551570} {:answer/id #uuid "f04ace2c-28da-5f40-9f65-67b8c99a6bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(6 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(- 4 z)\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551544} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6437e4-50bc-5914-9ed8-eb13c309f091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a201dc8-1f1f-5b87-b63b-68512a0298f7" :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}) \\\\  & =(6 x^{2})+(0)+(0) \\\\  & =6 x^{2} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}) \\\\  & =(0)+(6 y^{2})+(0) \\\\  & =6 y^{2} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}) \\\\  & =(0)+(0)+(4 z) \\\\  & =4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(6 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(6 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(4 z)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6f75a7-859f-5396-a003-9f5a5cf0ced9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 9 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(- 6 y)\\,\\mathbf j+(3 z^{2})\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551542} {:answer/id #uuid "33cfe26e-ed4b-597a-9860-326ba2f3203a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(- 6 y)\\,\\mathbf j+(- 3 z^{2})\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551547} {:answer/id #uuid "7796e127-39a7-5da3-9437-4640665f9876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(6 y)\\,\\mathbf j+(3 z^{2})\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551561} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f14adf-7f6f-5747-8d85-36b5baea3154" :answer/t